ter drums. Aviators adapted bubble sextants for use in aircraft. Electronic navigation and satellite systems, especially GPS, then displaced the sextant from daily use. It survives as a backup, a training tool, and a symbol of seafaring skill.\n  \n  WORKER_TOKEN=29537106\nagentId: a865953dd6e23c538 (use SendMessage with to: 'a865953dd6e23c538', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 5709\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 7716</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"303f7d7b-a953-48a8-ac9c-8a9298c6ad70","uuid":"82a013e3-d910-4b97-b9c2-dae4cbb8ce42","timestamp":"2026-11-18T11:50:24.359Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Number: 29537106. Topic: the history of the sextant.","agentId":"a865953dd6e23c538","agentType":"worker","toolsUsed":["Bash"],"harnessNoteCount":1,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"c53dd8e4fe2682fd","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<tools-used-before-summary>Bash</tools-used-before-summary>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"The sextant grew out of earlier tools for measuring the angle between a celestial body and the horizon. Sailors had long used the backstaff and the astrolabe, but both were awkward on a pitching ship. In 1731, John Hadley in England and Thomas Godfrey in Philadelphia independently invented the reflecting octant. It used two mirrors to bring the image of a star or the sun down to the horizon, so the observer could see both at once. That made measurements much steadier at sea.\n\nThe sextant followed in the 1750s. Captain John Campbell of the Royal Navy suggested extending the arc from one eighth of a circle to one sixth. That gave a wider range of measurable angles, which was essential for lunar distance observations. Jesse Ramsden later improved the manufacture of precise dividing engines, which made instruments more accurate and more widely available.\n\nThe sextant became the key partner of the marine chronometer. A navigator found latitude from the sun's noon altitude. Longitude came from comparing local time with the time at a reference meridian. Explorers such as James Cook relied on such tools to chart oceans with new precision.\n\nThrough the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, designs became lighter and more durable, with better telescopes and micrometer drums. Aviators adapted bubble sextants for use in aircraft. Electronic navigation and satellite systems, especially GPS, then displaced the sextant from daily use. It survives as a backup, a training tool, and a symbol of seafaring skill.\n\nWORKER_TOKEN=29537106"}],"resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","totalDurationMs":7716,"totalTokens":5709,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1743,"cache_read_input_tokens":3460,"output_tokens":504,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1743},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":504,"cache_read_input_tokens":3460,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1743,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1743,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":1,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0},"canContinueAgent":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EGFJjRyQAqBsw367Vv2H3j","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
