nd liquid-filled designs reduced needle swing. In the twentieth century, gyrocompasses, which find true north through rotation rather than magnetism, and later satellite navigation, reduced reliance on the magnetic compass, though it remains a trusted backup.\n  \n  WORKER_TOKEN=61840273\nagentId: adb23137a456de70d (use SendMessage with to: 'adb23137a456de70d', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 5736\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 8048</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"303f7d7b-a953-48a8-ac9c-8a9298c6ad70","uuid":"af0bad27-2523-4a1c-9ee4-589c710591a7","timestamp":"2026-11-18T11:50:24.368Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Number: 61840273. Topic: the history of the compass.","agentId":"adb23137a456de70d","agentType":"worker","toolsUsed":["Bash"],"harnessNoteCount":1,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"31e3d406bef802a2","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<tools-used-before-summary>Bash</tools-used-before-summary>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"The compass has a long history that begins in ancient China. Around the second century BCE, during the Han dynasty, people discovered that lodestone, a naturally magnetized iron ore, would align itself in a consistent direction. Early versions were not used for navigation at sea. Instead, they served in divination and geomancy, helping to arrange buildings and graves in harmony with cosmic principles. These early devices often used a spoon-shaped piece of lodestone that rotated on a polished bronze plate.\n\nBy the eleventh century, during the Song dynasty, Chinese writers described magnetized needles floating in water or suspended on a thread, and the instrument began to guide travelers and sailors. Shen Kuo recorded that such needles pointed slightly off true north, an early observation of magnetic declination.\n\nKnowledge of the compass reached Europe and the Islamic world by the late twelfth century, as shown in the writings of Alexander Neckam.